<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-derivatives-from-first-principles" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Derivatives from First Principles</title>
    <objectives component="outcomes">
        <introduction>
            <p>
            <dl>
                <li>
                    <title>Strand</title>
                    <p>
                        4.0 Calculus
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Sub-Strand</title>
                    <p>
                        4.2 Differentiation I
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Specific Learning Outcomes</title>
                    <p>
                        Determine the derivatives of polynomial functions
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Grade Level</title>
                    <p>
                        11
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Estimated Duration</title>
                    <p>
                        40 minutes
                    </p>
                </li>
            </dl>
            </p>
        </introduction>
    </objectives>

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Problem-Solving and Discovery</title>
        <dl>
            <li>
                <title>Suggested Time</title>
                <p>
                    10 minutes
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <title>Objective</title>
                <p>
                    Discover the derivative numerically — before any formula — by computing the AROC for progressively smaller values of <m>h</m> and noticing that the values converge to a fixed number.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <title>Teacher's Role</title>
                <p>
                    Do not use the word "derivative" until after the activity. Let learners observe and conjecture. Ask: "What value are the AROCs getting closer to?" and "Can you write a formula that shows exactly why?" Do not discuss part (c) as a class until most groups have completed the table.
                </p>
            </li>
        </dl>
    </axiom>

    <activity xml:id="activity-first-principles">
        <title>Getting Closer and Closer</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Consider the function <m>f(x) = x^2</m>. In the previous section we computed the average rate of change (AROC) between two points on a curve. Now let's see what happens when those two points get very close together.
            </p>

            <!-- TODO: TikZ figure — graph of f(x) = x² with three secant lines at x = 3 for h = 1, 0.5, 0.1, rotating toward the tangent at (3, 9) as h decreases; label each secant with its slope -->

            <p>
                Fix the first point at <m>x = 3</m> and let the second point be at <m>x = 3 + h</m>. Complete the table by calculating the AROC between these two points for each value of <m>h</m>.
            </p>

            <table xml:id="table-aroc-approaching-limit">
                <title>AROC of <m>f(x)=x^2</m> at <m>x=3</m> for decreasing <m>h</m></title>
                <tabular halign="center" top="minor" left="minor">
                    <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
                        <cell right="minor"><m>h</m></cell>
                        <cell right="minor"><m>f(3+h)</m></cell>
                        <cell right="minor"><m>f(3+h)-f(3)</m></cell>
                        <cell right="minor"><m>\dfrac{f(3+h)-f(3)}{h}</m></cell>
                    </row>
                    <row bottom="minor">
                        <cell right="minor"><m>1</m></cell>
                        <cell right="minor"><m>16</m></cell>
                        <cell right="minor"><m>7</m></cell>
                        <cell right="minor"><m>7</m></cell>
                    </row>
                    <row bottom="minor">
                        <cell right="minor"><m>0.5</m></cell>
                        <cell right="minor"><m>12.25</m></cell>
                        <cell right="minor"><m>3.25</m></cell>
                        <cell right="minor"><m>6.5</m></cell>
                    </row>
                    <row bottom="minor">
                        <cell right="minor"><m>0.1</m></cell>
                        <cell right="minor"><m>9.61</m></cell>
                        <cell right="minor"><m>0.61</m></cell>
                        <cell right="minor"><m>6.1</m></cell>
                    </row>
                    <row bottom="minor">
                        <cell right="minor"><m>0.01</m></cell>
                        <cell right="minor"><m>\phantom{00}</m></cell>
                        <cell right="minor"><m>\phantom{00}</m></cell>
                        <cell right="minor"><m>\phantom{00}</m></cell>
                    </row>
                    <row bottom="minor">
                        <cell right="minor"><m>0.001</m></cell>
                        <cell right="minor"><m>\phantom{00}</m></cell>
                        <cell right="minor"><m>\phantom{00}</m></cell>
                        <cell right="minor"><m>\phantom{00}</m></cell>
                    </row>
                </tabular>
            </table>

            <p>
                Use the Sage cell below to explore. Drag the slider to change <m>h</m> and watch the AROC update.
            </p>

            <sage>
                <input>
@interact
def _(h=slider(0.001, 1.0, 0.001, default=1.0, label='h')):
    f = lambda x: x**2
    a = 3
    aroc = (f(a + h) - f(a)) / h
    print(f"h        = {h:.3f}")
    print(f"f(3 + h) = f({a + h:.3f}) = {f(a + h):.6f}")
    print(f"AROC     = (f(3 + h) - f(3)) / h = {aroc:.6f}")
                </input>
            </sage>

            <p>
                Now ask Sage to compute the exact limit as <m>h \to 0</m>.
            </p>

            <sage>
                <input>
var('h')
f(x) = x^2
limit((f(3 + h) - f(3)) / h, h, 0)
                </input>
                <output>6</output>
            </sage>

            <ol marker="(a)">
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Complete the last two rows of the table. What pattern do you notice in the AROC column?
                    </p>
                    <solution component="lesson-plan">
                        <p>
                            AROC values: <m>6.01</m> and <m>6.001</m>. The values approach <m>6</m> from above.
                        </p>
                    </solution>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        What value does the AROC appear to approach as <m>h \to 0</m>?
                    </p>
                    <solution component="lesson-plan">
                        <p>
                            The AROC approaches <m>6</m>.
                        </p>
                    </solution>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Write a simplified algebraic formula for the AROC of <m>f(x) = x^2</m> between <m>x = 3</m> and <m>x = 3 + h</m>. Use it to explain the pattern in part (a).
                    </p>
                    <solution component="lesson-plan">
                        <p>
                            <md>
                                <mrow>\text{AROC} \amp= \frac{f(3+h)-f(3)}{h} = \frac{(3+h)^2 - 9}{h}</mrow>
                                <mrow>\amp= \frac{9 + 6h + h^2 - 9}{h} = \frac{6h + h^2}{h} = 6 + h</mrow>
                            </md>
                            Since AROC <m>= 6 + h</m>, as <m>h \to 0</m> the AROC approaches <m>6</m>.
                        </p>
                    </solution>
                    <note component="lesson-plan">
                        <p>
                            Learners who reach <m>\text{AROC} = 6 + h</m> have essentially computed the derivative — they just haven't named it yet. Ask: "So what is the <em>exact</em> slope of the tangent to <m>f(x) = x^2</m> at <m>x = 3</m>?"
                        </p>
                    </note>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        What does this limiting value represent on the graph of <m>f</m>?
                    </p>
                    <solution component="lesson-plan">
                        <p>
                            It is the slope of the tangent to <m>f(x) = x^2</m> at the point <m>(3, 9)</m>. As <m>h \to 0</m>, the secant line through <m>(3, 9)</m> and <m>(3+h, f(3+h))</m> rotates toward the tangent — exactly the picture in the previous section.
                        </p>
                    </solution>
                </li>
            </ol>
        </statement>
    </activity>

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Structured Instruction</title>
        <p>
            <dl>
                <li>
                    <title>Suggested Time</title>
                    <p>
                        5 minutes
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Objective</title>
                    <p>
                        Formalise the limit process learners discovered in the activity as the definition of the derivative.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </dl>
        </p>
        <paragraphs>
            <title>Key Takeaways &amp; Teacher Connection</title>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        <alert>Connect to the activity:</alert> In part (c), learners found AROC <m>= 6 + h</m>. The derivative at <m>x = 3</m> is exactly what remains as <m>h \to 0</m>: the value <m>6</m>.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Replacing the specific input <m>x = 3</m> with a general <m>x</m> produces a formula that works everywhere — the derivative <em>function</em> <m>f'(x)</m>.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Stress the three-step procedure: compute <m>f(x+h)</m>, simplify <m>\frac{f(x+h)-f(x)}{h}</m>, take the limit. This is the algorithm for all examples that follow.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </paragraphs>
    </axiom>

    <insight xml:id="insight-first-principles-key-ideas">
        <title>From AROC to the Derivative</title>
        <p>
            The activity showed that as <m>h \to 0</m>, the AROC of <m>f(x) = x^2</m> at <m>x = 3</m> converges to the value <m>6</m>. Two key observations:
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <p>
                    This limiting value is the <term>slope of the tangent</term> to the curve at <m>x = 3</m> — the instantaneous rate of change at that point.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Repeating the process with a general input <m>x</m> instead of <m>x = 3</m> produces a <em>function</em> <m>f'(x)</m>, giving the slope at <em>every</em> point. This is the <term>derivative</term> of <m>f</m>.
                </p>
            </li>
        </ul>
    </insight>

    <p>Now that we can find the average rate of change, suppose we make <m>h</m> in <xref ref="PRECALC-h2P97umVs7"/> smaller and smaller. Then <m>a+h</m> will approach <m>a</m> as <m>h</m> gets smaller, getting closer and closer to 0. Likewise, the second point <m>(a+h,f(a+h))</m> will approach the first point, <m>(a,f(a)).</m> As a consequence, the connecting line between the two points, called the secant line, will get closer and closer to being a tangent to the function at <m>x=a,</m> and the slope of the secant line will get closer and closer to the slope of the tangent at <m>x=a.</m> See <xref ref="PRECALC-YRVPEZzgU0"/>.</p>

    <figure xml:id="PRECALC-YRVPEZzgU0" original="PRECALC/modules/m49455/index.cnxml" license="CC-BY-4.0">
        <caption>The connecting line between two points moves closer to being a tangent line at <m>x=a.</m>
        </caption>
        <image source="PRECALC/CNX_Precalc_Figure_12_04_003.jpg" width="80%"/>
    </figure>

    <p>Because we are looking for the <term>slope of the tangent</term> at <m>x=a,</m> we can think of the measure of the slope of the curve of a function <m>f</m> at a given point as the rate of change at a particular instant. We call this slope the <em>instantaneous rate of change</em>, or the <em>derivative</em> of the function at <m>x=a.</m> Both can be found by finding the limit of the slope of a line connecting the point at <m>x=a</m> with a second point infinitesimally close along the curve. For a function <m>f</m> both the instantaneous rate of change of the function and the derivative of the function at <m>x=a</m> are written as <m>f'(a),</m> and we can define them as a <term>two-sided limit</term> that has the same value whether approached from the left or the right.</p>

    <p>
        <me>f'(a)=\lim_{h \to 0}\frac{f(a+h)-f(a)}{h}</me>
    </p>

    <p>The expression by which the limit is found is known as the <term>difference quotient</term>.</p>

    <insight xml:id="PRECALC-fmeXCOah2m" original="PRECALC/modules/m49455/index.cnxml" license="CC-BY-4.0">
        <title>Definition of Instantaneous Rate of Change and Derivative</title>
        <p>The <term>derivative</term>, or <term>instantaneous rate of change</term>, of a function <m>f</m> at <m>x=a,</m> is given by</p>
        <p>
            <me>f'(a)=\lim_{h \to 0}\frac{f(a+h)-f(a)}{h}</me>
        </p>
        <p>The expression <m>\frac{f(a+h)-f(a)}{h}</m> is called the difference quotient.</p>
        <p>We use the difference quotient to evaluate the limit of the rate of change of the function as <m>h</m> approaches 0.</p>
    </insight>

    <paragraphs>
        <title>Derivatives: Interpretations and Notation</title>
        <p>The <term>derivative</term> of a function can be interpreted in different ways.</p>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <p>The derivative of a function <m>f(x)</m> at a point <m>x=a</m> is the slope of the tangent line to the curve <m>f(x)</m> at <m>x=a.</m> The derivative of <m>f(x)</m> at <m>x=a</m> is written <m>f'(a).</m></p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>The derivative <m>f'(a)</m> measures how the curve changes at the point <m>(a,f(a)).</m></p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>The derivative <m>f'(a)</m> may be thought of as the instantaneous rate of change of the function <m>f(x)</m> at <m>x=a.</m></p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>If a function measures distance as a function of time, then the derivative measures the instantaneous <term>velocity</term> at time <m>t=a.</m></p>
            </li>
        </ul>
    </paragraphs>

    <insight xml:id="PRECALC-2h816Bh09j" original="PRECALC/modules/m49455/index.cnxml" license="CC-BY-4.0">
        <title>Notations for the Derivative</title>
        <p>The equation of the derivative of a function <m>f(x)</m> is written as <m>y'=f'(x),</m> where <m>y=f(x).</m> The notation <m>f'(x)</m> is read as "<m>f</m> prime of <m>x</m>." Alternate notations for the derivative include the following:</p>
        <p>
            <me>f'(x)=y'=\frac{dy}{dx}=\frac{df}{dx}=\frac{d}{dx}f(x)</me>
        </p>
        <p>The expression <m>f'(x)</m> is now a function of <m>x</m>; this function gives the slope of the curve <m>y=f(x)</m> at any value of <m>x.</m> The derivative of a function <m>f(x)</m> at a point <m>x=a</m> is denoted <m>f'(a).</m>
        </p>
    </insight>

    <insight xml:id="PRECALC-7rXzYVguNZ" original="PRECALC/modules/m49455/index.cnxml" license="CC-BY-4.0">
        <title>Finding the Derivative</title>
        <p>Given a function <m>f,</m> find the derivative by applying the definition of the derivative.</p>
        <ol>
            <li>
                <p>Calculate <m>f(x+h).</m></p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>Substitute and simplify <m>\dfrac{f(x+h)-f(x)}{h}.</m></p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>Evaluate the limit: <m>f'(x)=\lim_{h \to 0}\dfrac{f(x+h)-f(x)}{h}.</m></p>
            </li>
        </ol>
    </insight>

    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/example-intro.ptx" />

    <example xml:id="example-fp-linear">
        <title>Derivative of a Linear Function</title>
        <statement>
            <p>Find <m>f'(x)</m> from first principles for <m>f(x) = 2x + 5</m>.</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                <alert>Step 1.</alert> Compute <m>f(x+h)</m>.
                <me>f(x+h) = 2(x+h)+5 = 2x + 2h + 5</me>
            </p>
            <p>
                <alert>Step 2.</alert> Form the difference quotient and simplify.
                <md>
                    <mrow>\frac{f(x+h)-f(x)}{h} \amp= \frac{(2x+2h+5)-(2x+5)}{h} = \frac{2h}{h} = 2</mrow>
                </md>
            </p>
            <p>
                <alert>Step 3.</alert> Take the limit.
                <me>f'(x) = \lim_{h \to 0}\, 2 = 2</me>
            </p>
            <p>
                The derivative of a linear function equals its slope — constant everywhere.
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="example-fp-quadratic">
        <title>Derivative of a Quadratic</title>
        <statement>
            <p>Find <m>f'(x)</m> from first principles for <m>f(x) = x^2</m>.</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                <alert>Step 1.</alert> Compute <m>f(x+h)</m>.
                <me>f(x+h) = (x+h)^2 = x^2 + 2xh + h^2</me>
            </p>
            <p>
                <alert>Step 2.</alert> Form the difference quotient and simplify.
                <md>
                    <mrow>\frac{f(x+h)-f(x)}{h} \amp= \frac{x^2+2xh+h^2 - x^2}{h}</mrow>
                    <mrow>\amp= \frac{2xh + h^2}{h} = \frac{h(2x+h)}{h} = 2x + h</mrow>
                </md>
            </p>
            <p>
                <alert>Step 3.</alert> Take the limit.
                <me>f'(x) = \lim_{h \to 0}(2x+h) = 2x</me>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="example-fp-poly-at-point">
        <title>Derivative of a Polynomial, Evaluated at a Point</title>
        <statement>
            <p>Find <m>f'(x)</m> from first principles for <m>f(x) = x^2 - 3x</m>, and hence find <m>f'(2)</m>.</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                <alert>Step 1.</alert> Compute <m>f(x+h)</m>.
                <me>f(x+h) = (x+h)^2 - 3(x+h) = x^2 + 2xh + h^2 - 3x - 3h</me>
            </p>
            <p>
                <alert>Step 2.</alert> Form the difference quotient and simplify.
                <md>
                    <mrow>\frac{f(x+h)-f(x)}{h} \amp= \frac{(x^2+2xh+h^2-3x-3h)-(x^2-3x)}{h}</mrow>
                    <mrow>\amp= \frac{2xh + h^2 - 3h}{h} = \frac{h(2x+h-3)}{h} = 2x + h - 3</mrow>
                </md>
            </p>
            <p>
                <alert>Step 3.</alert> Take the limit.
                <me>f'(x) = \lim_{h \to 0}(2x+h-3) = 2x - 3</me>
            </p>
            <p>
                Substituting <m>x = 2</m>:
                <me>f'(2) = 2(2) - 3 = 1</me>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/assessment-intro.ptx" />

    <exercises xml:id="exercises-first-principles">

        <exercise xml:id="ex-fp-1">
            <statement>
                <p>Find <m>f'(x)</m> from first principles for <m>f(x) = 3x - 7</m>.</p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p><m>f'(x) = 3</m></p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-fp-2">
            <statement>
                <p>Find <m>f'(x)</m> from first principles for <m>f(x) = x^2 + 4</m>.</p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p><m>f'(x) = 2x</m></p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-fp-3">
            <statement>
                <p>Find <m>f'(x)</m> from first principles for <m>f(x) = x^2 + 5x</m>.</p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    <md>
                        <mrow>\frac{f(x+h)-f(x)}{h} \amp= \frac{(x+h)^2+5(x+h)-(x^2+5x)}{h}</mrow>
                        <mrow>\amp= \frac{2xh + h^2 + 5h}{h} = 2x + h + 5</mrow>
                    </md>
                    Taking the limit: <m>f'(x) = 2x + 5</m>.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-fp-4">
            <statement>
                <p>Using the definition of the derivative, find <m>f'(1)</m> for <m>f(x) = x^3</m>.</p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    <md>
                        <mrow>\frac{f(1+h)-f(1)}{h} \amp= \frac{(1+h)^3 - 1}{h}</mrow>
                        <mrow>\amp= \frac{1 + 3h + 3h^2 + h^3 - 1}{h}</mrow>
                        <mrow>\amp= \frac{3h + 3h^2 + h^3}{h} = 3 + 3h + h^2</mrow>
                    </md>
                    As <m>h \to 0</m>: <m>f'(1) = 3</m>.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-fp-5">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    A stone is thrown upward and its height (in metres) after <m>t</m> seconds is <m>s(t) = 20t - 5t^2</m>.
                    Use first principles to find its instantaneous velocity at <m>t = 1</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    <md>
                        <mrow>\frac{s(1+h)-s(1)}{h} \amp= \frac{20(1+h)-5(1+h)^2 - 15}{h}</mrow>
                        <mrow>\amp= \frac{20+20h-5-10h-5h^2 - 15}{h}</mrow>
                        <mrow>\amp= \frac{10h - 5h^2}{h} = 10 - 5h</mrow>
                    </md>
                    As <m>h \to 0</m>: instantaneous velocity <m>= 10</m> m/s.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-fp-6">
            <statement>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li>
                        <p>If <m>f(x) = c</m> is a constant function, use the definition of the derivative to show that <m>f'(x) = 0</m>.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>What does <m>f'(a) = 0</m> mean geometrically?</p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Since <m>f(x+h) = c</m>, the difference quotient is
                            <me>\frac{f(x+h)-f(x)}{h} = \frac{c - c}{h} = 0.</me>
                            Therefore <m>f'(x) = \lim_{h \to 0} 0 = 0</m>.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            The tangent to the curve at <m>x = a</m> is horizontal — the curve is neither rising nor falling at that point.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </solution>
        </exercise>

    </exercises>

